  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nA secondary cold front moving in from the northwest will kick\noff another round of showers and storms across the forecast \narea this afternoon. With dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s \nand temperatures rising into the mid 80s, conditions are ripe \nfor a few storms with heavy rain. MUCAPE is 2000-3000 J/kg and \nthere is moderate shear around 40 kts, so cannot rule out \nisolated damaging wind gusts, but confidence is low in severe \nweather. However, this front will sag to the south-southwest \nthrough the evening and subsidence and cooler/drier air will \nfollow, suppressing convection.\n\nWinds will shift to the north behind the front tonight, and lake\nenhanced clouds and showers are expected to develop off of \nsouthern Lake Michigan late tonight and into Wednesday with the \ncooler air aloft and warmer lake temperatures in place. Lows on \nWednesday \nmorning will be in the 50s while parts of Lake Michigan surface \nwater temperatures are currently in the lower 60s to lower 70s. \nNot expecting much of accumulation from any of the showers but \nwill be enough to keep areas near the lake damp on Wednesday.\nOtherwise mild and dry conditions will prevail through Saturday\nwith the storm track staying south of the area. High pressure\nwill start to build in on Sunday and temperature will warm back\ninto the upper 80s. Moisture will also return with a chance for\nshowers and storms Sunday night and Monday as a series of\nshortwaves is forecast to ride over the top of the ridge into\nthe Great Lakes and Ohio Valley.",
